RISK_NAMES = {'Risk Neutral':'Risk-neutral', 'Moderately Risk Averse':'Moderately risk-averse',
              'Mildly Risk Averse':'Mildly risk-averse', 'Highly Risk Averse':'Highly risk-averse',
              'P Risk Averse':'Only the plaintiff is risk-averse', 'D Risk Averse':'Only the defendant is risk-averse',
              'P More Risk Averse':'The plaintiff is more risk-averse', 'D More Risk Averse':'The defendant is more risk-averse'}
COST_NAMES = {'0.25':'one-quarter litigation costs','1':'baseline litigation costs','4':'four-times litigation costs','0':'zero litigation costs'}

def outcome_name(key): return OUTCOMES[key][0]
def parameter_name(key): return PARAMETERS.get(key,(key,''))[0]

def setting_name(case, baseline):
    p=case['Parameters']
    parts=[RISK_NAMES.get(p['Risk Aversion'],p['Risk Aversion']),COST_NAMES.get(p['Costs Multiplier'],'litigation cost factor '+p['Costs Multiplier']),
           'fee reimbursement '+p['Fee Shifting Multiplier'], 'damages '+p['Damages Multiplier'], 'liability standard '+p['Liability Threshold']]
    for key,value in p.items():
        if key not in ('Risk Aversion','Costs Multiplier','Fee Shifting Multiplier','Damages Multiplier','Liability Threshold') and value!=baseline.get(key):
            parts.append(parameter_name(key).lower()+': '+value)
    return '; '.join(parts)

MODEL_HTML = '''<p>This is a model of how the prospect of a lawsuit changes behavior <em>before</em> an accident, as well as what happens afterward. The potential injurer is called the defendant; the potential victim is called the plaintiff, even before anyone files suit.</p>
<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Choose whether to act and how much care to take.</strong> The defendant has an opportunity to do something valuable, such as driving or operating a business. The defendant can decline, or participate and choose a level of precaution. Care costs resources but reduces accident risk.</li><li><strong>An accident—or a mistaken attribution—may create a dispute.</strong> The parties have imperfect information about how effective care would have been. The defendant can also be blamed for an injury the defendant did not cause.</li><li><strong>Choose whether to litigate.</strong> The plaintiff decides whether to file. If a suit is filed, the defendant decides whether to contest it or default. These decisions cost money.</li><li><strong>Try to settle.</strong> Each side submits a monetary offer without seeing the other side’s offer. If the offers meet or cross, the case settles. Otherwise, the plaintiff may abandon the case and the defendant may default.</li><li><strong>Proceed to trial if neither side exits.</strong> The court uses imperfect information to decide liability. Damages and any applicable fee reimbursement follow.</li></ol>
<p>Changing a legal rule can affect every step. Fewer trials might mean more settlements, fewer accidents, fewer filings, or more defaults. That is why this report does not treat any one litigation rate as a complete measure of success.</p>'''

COST_HTML = '''<p><strong>Lower total social cost is better under the paper’s resource-based welfare measure.</strong> The total has four parts: resources spent on care; the benefit lost when an activity is not undertaken; physical accident losses; and resources spent on litigation.</p><p>A damages payment or settlement transfers money from one side to the other. It is not itself a loss to society in this accounting, although its incentive effects can change real costs. Fee reimbursement likewise changes who pays; the underlying legal work still consumes resources.</p><p>The charts combine care and forgone activity in one segment. The lost activity benefit is already offset by the absence of the care, harm and litigation costs that nonparticipation avoids. The model uses the same activity benefit for everyone, assumes it captures the activity’s social value, and offers an all-or-nothing participation decision. Those are substantive simplifications. The welfare total also excludes a separate value for avoiding financial risk, even when parties are risk-averse.</p>'''

METHODS_HTML = '''<p>An <strong>equilibrium</strong> is a pattern of behavior in which neither side has a worthwhile reason to change its own plan, given the other side’s behavior and the information available. The computer searches for approximately stable behavior. Different searches or different rules for choosing among stable patterns can produce different outcomes.</p><div class="three"><article><h3>Original draft</h3><p>The one equilibrium reported for each setting in the draft’s published results file. “First equilibrium” here means that original published result; it is not the first of the ten new searches.</p></article><article><h3>Gini approach</h3><p>Among joint mixtures of plans that satisfy the incentive checks, this rule seeks to spread probability rather than concentrate it on a few pairs of plans. “Gini” names the mathematical measure of that spread. It does not mean that the rule maximizes social welfare or equality of the parties’ payoffs.</p></article><article><h3>Liu-affinity approach</h3><p>This rule first measures how similar plans are in their economic consequences, using both parties’ payoffs and the rates of filing, contesting and trial against fixed test opponents. It constructs reference weights that account for that similarity, then seeks a stable mixture close to those weights. It does not select the socially best result or come with a guarantee of selecting a Nash equilibrium.</p></article></div><p>For each setting, each new approach begins from ten independently initialized searches. The final calculation gathers the plans discovered in those searches, including documented repairs, and chooses fresh probabilities over the combined collection. This is called <strong>pooling</strong>. It is not an average of the ten outcomes, and finding a plan more often does not automatically give it more weight. Both approaches finish with the same collection of plans available for that setting.</p><p>The comparison changes the equilibrium concept as well as the selection rule. The new approaches permit correlation between complete plans. The draft uses its original Nash-equilibrium calculation. A difference between them cannot therefore be attributed only to choosing a different Nash equilibrium. <a href="methods.html#equilibrium">Read the distinction in plain English</a>.</p>'''

CHART_HTML = '''<p>Each group of panels changes one legal rule while holding the other displayed model assumptions fixed. Columns marked quarter, baseline and four-times costs change only the expense of litigating. They are hypothetical environments, not estimates for particular jurisdictions.</p><p>The grey line is the original draft; teal is Gini; orange is Liu. A lower total-cost line uses fewer resources overall. More care is not automatically better: its cost must be weighed against the accidents it prevents. A flatter line means the rule changes that outcome relatively little in the illustrated environment.</p><p>Costs are shown per 10,000 opportunities to undertake the activity, with the harm from one physical accident set to one model unit. These are not dollar estimates. The average care level uses the model’s scale from 0 to 7; nonparticipation contributes zero.</p>'''

DENOMINATOR_HTML = '''<p><strong>“Among all opportunities” and “among disputes” answer different questions.</strong> An opportunity is a chance to undertake the activity, even if the defendant declines or nothing goes wrong. A dispute is an actual or wrongly attributed accident; it need not become a lawsuit.</p><p>Suppose 100,000 opportunities produce 10 disputes and 4 settlements. That is 4 settlements per 100,000 opportunities, but 40% of disputes. If a rule prevents accidents, the first rate can fall while the second rises. Neither change alone tells us whether total social cost improved.</p><p>In these charts, contesting and settling “among disputes” use all disputes as the denominator—not just filed lawsuits. Abandonment and default are separate ways a case ends and are not counted as settlements. Rates are undefined when there are no disputes; a blank is not a zero.</p>'''

ACCURACY_HTML = '''<p><strong>A small reported error is not always a small economic incentive.</strong> The computer asks how much a party could improve its expected payoff by changing its plan. It then divides that gain by the full range of possible utility values in the game. This division is called normalization. Utility represents the party’s preferences, including its attitude to financial risk.</p><p>For some risk-averse settings, very costly possible outcomes make that range enormous. Dividing by this enormous number can make a meaningful profitable change look tiny. For illustration, 1 divided by a billion is a tiny ratio, but the original gain is still 1. Thus, passing the ratio-based stopping rule does not necessarily mean the party has little economic reason to change behavior.</p><p>This matters for nine high-cost, moderately risk-averse settings with double fee reimbursement and double damages. Across their 18 Gini and Liu outputs, the defendant’s available improvement is worth about <strong>35–123% of the benefit from undertaking the activity</strong>. To compare unlike utility scales, we express that improvement as the change in a guaranteed amount of money that would have the same utility effect—a <strong>certainty-equivalent gain</strong>.</p><p>Those improvements may involve other choices besides participation. We have not proved that the reported reductions in participation are artifacts. But we cannot yet treat them as reliable equilibrium predictions. Agreement between Gini, Liu and repeated searches does not solve a limitation they share. Some conclusions about risk, total cost and variability also need reassessment. The problem is uneven: the reviewed risk-neutral pooled defendant gains are all below 0.1% of the activity benefit.</p><p>This incentive measure is not a bound on error in social cost. The ten initial searches still need a separate review in these economic units. In variants where parties misperceive costs, the measure concerns their perceived wealth. The existing accounting, records and formal pass/fail labels are preserved; a more economically demanding accuracy check is additional work.</p>'''

GLOSSARY = [
 ('Setting','One specified combination of legal rules, litigation costs, information quality, care costs and attitudes toward risk. It is a modeled scenario, not an observed lawsuit.'),
 ('Plan or policy','A complete strategy telling a party what to do at each possible decision and information state. In the algorithm, “policy” means a strategy; elsewhere in the report, a “legal policy” means a legal rule.'),
 ('Independent start','A fresh numerical search with a different initialization. Ten starts explore sensitivity to that starting point; they are not ten real-world observations.'),
 ('Matched comparison','Two settings that differ only in the named parameter or explicitly identified package. Missing combinations are not filled in by guessing.'),
 ('Baseline','The reference assumptions used to construct a comparison. Baseline does not mean empirically correct or socially optimal.'),
 ('Median','The middle observation after values are ordered. Half are at or below it and half at or above it, subject to ties.'),
 ('90th percentile','A value at or below which about 90% of the observations lie. It describes the larger cases; it is not a 90% probability that a conclusion is correct.'),
 ('Percentage point','The difference between two percentages. Moving from 10% to 15% is five percentage points, but a 50% relative increase.'),
 ('Risk-neutral / risk-averse','Risk-neutral parties care about expected money. Risk-averse parties also dislike uncertainty in their wealth.'),
 ('Pool or population','The collection of plans available to the numerical search, not a population of people.'),
 ('Incentive gain / deviation gain','The amount by which one party could improve its expected payoff by changing its plan while the comparison behavior is held fixed.'),
 ('Tolerance','The maximum incentive gain or numerical error a test permits. A result passing a tolerance is approximate, not exact.'),
 ('Audit','An independent calculation or integrity check. Passing a file check, an incentive check and a check of the selector’s objective are different achievements.'),
 ('Hash / SHA256','A digital fingerprint used to check that a source file has not changed. It identifies evidence; it does not establish an economic conclusion.'),
]
